Q: Worst way to combine six or less byte arrays in JavaScript A: <p>In c#12 they introduced the <code>Collection Expressions</code> feature which you can read more about <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/operators/collection-expressions" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Here</a></p>
<pre><code>public static byte[] Combine(this byte[] source, byte[] first, byte[] second) 
{
    return [.. source, .. first, .. second];
}
</code></pre>
<p>Alternative using <code>Collection Expressions</code> you could use this extension for any amount of byte[] arrays.</p>
<pre><code>public static byte[] Combine(this byte[] source, params byte[][] arrays)
{            
    if (arrays == null || arrays.Length == 0)
        return source;
    byte[] result = source;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arrays.Length; i++) 
    {
        result = [..result, ..arrays[i]];
    }
    return result;
}
</code></pre>
